Subject: [PATCH 2/2] atmel_z500: keep the touch controller awake in recovery

The tail of mxt_probe() puts the digitiser to sleep whenever the kernel was not
booted into the main OS:

    if(entry_mode != 1) {
            msleep(1000);
            mxt_set_t7_power_cfg(data, MXT_POWER_CFG_DEEPSLEEP);
            touch_ic_status = false;
            return 0;
    }

init/main.c documents the values in ASUS' own comment: 1 = MOS, 2 = recovery,
3 = POS, 4 = COS. With touch_ic_status false the interrupt handler drops every
event, so the touchscreen is dead in recovery -- which is why the stock recovery
is driven with the volume keys, and why a custom recovery looks like it has hung.

Keeping the controller asleep is reasonable for charger mode, where the point is
not to burn battery while the screen is off. It makes no sense in recovery, whose
entire interface is a touchscreen. So treat recovery like the main OS and leave
the other two modes alone.

This replaces a 3-byte patch of the compiled image (tools/patch-kernel.py in the
TWRP device tree), which rewrote the compiler's "b.eq" into an unconditional
branch and therefore skipped the block in *every* mode, charger included. In the
source it is both narrower and legible.

entry_mode itself must not be patched globally: the charger, display and sensor
drivers branch on it too -- mdss_dsi_iris2.c and mdss_dsi_iris2_lightup.c have
their own "entry_mode == 2" paths for the Pixelworks bridge -- and forcing it
panics the kernel at boot. Only this one site may be touched.
---
diff --git drivers/input/touchscreen/atmel_z500/atmel_mxt_ts_1666t2.c drivers/input/touchscreen/atmel_z500/atmel_mxt_ts_1666t2.c
index 78bf844e..a441fc3f 100755
--- drivers/input/touchscreen/atmel_z500/atmel_mxt_ts_1666t2.c
+++ drivers/input/touchscreen/atmel_z500/atmel_mxt_ts_1666t2.c
@@ -6152,7 +6152,14 @@ static int mxt_probe(struct i2c_client *client, const struct i2c_device_id *id)
 	LDBG(" ++ success touch_ic_status: %d\n",touch_ic_status);
 	
 	/* Add by Tom Cheng for Check is entry_mode */
-	if(entry_mode != 1) {
+	/*
+	 * entry_mode: 1 = MOS, 2 = recovery, 3 = POS, 4 = COS (init/main.c).
+	 * Sleeping the controller saves battery in charger mode, but in recovery
+	 * it leaves the digitiser dead -- with touch_ic_status false the IRQ
+	 * handler drops every event -- and recovery is driven by touch. So treat
+	 * recovery like the main OS.
+	 */
+	if(entry_mode != 1 && entry_mode != 2) {
 		msleep(1000); //add dely time to fix cos hang by tom (time issue)
 		// Add by Tom Cheng for enter suspend mode
 		//touch_ic_status = true;
